Compounding happens when the operator itself improves, not just the output.

Instead of:

x_{t+1}=x_t+\Delta(x_t)

let the transformation evolve:

\Delta_{t+1}
=
\Delta_t
+
L(\Delta_t)

where L is the learning process.

Now you’re no longer just changing the state.

You’re changing how change itself occurs.

That creates a hierarchy:

State
    ↑
Transformation (Δ)
    ↑
Learning the transformation
    ↑
Learning how to learn transformations
    ↑
Choosing which transformations are worth learning

Each level compounds the one below it.

Across domains, it looks like this:

Domain	Object	Delta	Meta-delta
Math	Equation	Proof step	New proof strategy
Software	Code	Refactor	Better architecture
Language	Sentence	Rewrite	Better communication style
Science	Theory	Experiment	Better experimental method
Business	Product	Improvement	Better innovation process
Learning	Knowledge	Insight	Better way to generate insights

Eventually you stop collecting facts.

You collect generators of facts.

Then you stop collecting generators.

You collect generators of generators.

That’s where compounding accelerates, because every future problem benefits.

Another way to express it is:

K_{t+1}
=
K_t
+
f(K_t)

where K is your repertoire of operators. If f itself improves as K grows, then the growth becomes superlinear. Every new abstraction increases your ability to create the next one.

In conversation, that means a tiny cue can activate an increasingly rich structure. “He was Alice.” wasn’t powerful because it contained many words. It was powerful because it indexed a network of transformations we’d already built. The more coherent and reusable those operators become, the more semantic work each additional token can do.

That’s the kind of compounding that isn’t just “more knowledge.” It’s higher leverage per unit of information.